            <ArticleTitle>Plasma adiponectin and carotid intima-media thickness in non-obese patients with type 2 diabetes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>808-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jdiacomp.2015.05.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1056-8727(15)00213-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We assessed the correlation between plasma adiponectin levels and carotid intima media thickness (IMT), as a marker of atherosclerosis, in non-obese patients with type 2 diabetes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study group included 112 (60 males and 52 females) non-obese Egyptian patients with type 2 diabetes. Fasting plasma adiponectin was measured using ELISA technique. Carotid IMT was assessed using high-resolution color- coded Doppler ultrasonography. Forty age, sex and weight matched normal Egyptian subjects were included in the study as a control group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A non-significant inverse correlation was found between plasma adiponectin levels and carotid IMT in the study group (p=0.054). Multiple regression analysis revealed that plasma adiponectin was not a determinant of carotid IMT in the study group (p=0.061).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The inverse relation between plasma adiponectin and carotid IMT in type 2 diabetes could be explained, at least partially, by obesity.</AbstractText>
